C2/D2 Independent Target Pulsar Journal namespace and first-send recovery fence

Date: 2026-10-07
Base: main@4fcbaa884c01120d86b46e8058e379590410cc49

Target Journal v1 has an independent closed codec/hash namespace and freezes full immutable Target channel, physical Target, sequence, Message/generation/attempt, prepared/template/artifact hashes and exact Admission source. Legacy Lane generation-3 codec/bytes remain unchanged; the old ProducerKey constructor/accessors remain supported. Producer stable scope excludes normal channel/credential renewal so the sequence does not reset; frozen attempts require the full original channel. Evidence-generation changes require explicit sequence-domain migration. Target attempt lookup uses an indexed bounded map and forbids changed/duplicate mappings.

The existing four-state machine is reused. Target append exception/null/non-monotonic acknowledgement fences the instance before another mapping/sequence/SEND can be admitted. Finite retained record/byte limits and closed root-field bounds are enforced; replay failure fences partial state. A fresh durable ownership marker permits one process-local first-send token, consumed before the sender; replay, idempotent markers and exceptions never mint/return it. The pre-ownership legacy SEND entrance is unavailable in Target mode. Target cursor/checkpoint/Floor/GC remains unimplemented.

Source-locked openTarget uses a distinct protected resource, mandatory Writer/namespace guard before creation, after complete replay, before append and after ACK/readback, and an Exclusive Journal producer. It reuses guarded ACK validation, exact response-loss readback and contiguous startup tail replay. Target readback is bounded too. The mapping overload in the record factory checks full Source/Message/payload/template/artifact/channel equality; DTOs alone remain insufficient Writer or durability authority.

